import redox_ast::Pattern

-- | Whether this pattern is safe beside another without parentheses.
-- |
-- | An `Apply` with **no** arguments — `None`, `Close` — is a bare name and
-- | needs nothing, which is why the arity is tested rather than the variant.
-- |
-- | **And the name must be one segment.** `Some Kind::Called` does not parse:
-- | the parser's pattern-argument reader takes a bare name and not a path, so
-- | a path pattern still needs its parentheses. Saying `Binding -> true` made
-- | `redox fmt` strip them and write source its own parser refuses — the fifth
-- | time a round-trip has found that, after plan 69's three and plan 79's one,
-- | and the second where the formatter was the half at fault.
-- |
-- | **It is the `Binding` arm and not the `Apply` one**, which is the part
-- | that took two attempts: `pattern_app` keeps a bare constructor as a
-- | `Pattern::Binding` carrying the whole path — *"a bare constructor stays a
-- | `Binding`, as it was before this plan"* — so a zero-argument `Apply` is a
-- | shape the parser never builds and guarding it changed nothing. Plan 81 §2
-- | records it; `binding_text` in `redox-emit` is where it surfaced.
export(crate) stands_alone : &Pattern -> bool
stands_alone pattern = case pattern of
  Pattern::Apply { path, args, .. } -> .is_empty args && !.contains "::" path
  Pattern::Binding ident -> !.contains "::" ident.text
  Pattern::Literal { .. } or Pattern::Wildcard _ or Pattern::Tuple { .. } or Pattern::Slice { .. } or Pattern::Rest _ or Pattern::NamedRest { .. } -> true
  Pattern::Range { .. } or Pattern::Record { .. } or Pattern::As { .. } or Pattern::Or _ -> false
